WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.400
Disney's Alice in Wonderland is a magical journey&nbsp;
into nonsense. But for all the childish delight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.320
this classic animated movie has to offer, it also&nbsp;
has a more grown-up side. From its philosophical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.320
origins to its love of the psychedelic, these are&nbsp;
things only adults notice in Alice in Wonderland.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.360
If you watch Alice in Wonderland as a grown-up, it&nbsp;
becomes immediately apparent just how unsupervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.320
Alice actually is. Although her sister appears to&nbsp;
be old enough to look out for the both of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.440
the lack of any parental presence&nbsp;
throughout the course of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.080
might prompt you to wonder just where all&nbsp;
the responsible adults are supposed to be.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.689
When the film hit theaters in the early 1950s&nbsp;
— in fact, until relatively recently — free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:47.760
range children were the norm, rather&nbsp;
than the exception. used to be told to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.160
play outside with other neighborhood children&nbsp;
and come home when the streetlights came on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.280
Entire hours would go by where the only&nbsp;
parent any kid encountered was Mother Nature.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.040
And while the modernization of society generally&nbsp;
comes with its improvement, modern times also have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.680
their own problems and challenges. Thanks&nbsp;
to everything from infectious diseases to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.120
advancements in computing, kids nowadays generally&nbsp;
spend more time inside than their predecessors —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.240
for better or for worse. Either way, Alice&nbsp;
certainly has the edge on modern youngsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.600
here... and maybe it's not so surprising that she&nbsp;
finds it so easy to wander off the beaten path.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.280
Before he put pen to paper, Lewis Carroll's&nbsp;
stories originated as oral tales he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.640
regale to colleagues and their children, while&nbsp;
boating between Oxford and Woodstock — two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.240
locations rooted deep in English history. Eager&nbsp;
to entertain older minds as well as younger ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.040
Carroll took inspiration from his surroundings&nbsp;
and his experiences in higher education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.080
layering them into his stories as they developed.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.800
A palpable sense of childish fancy is&nbsp;
plainly apparent in his stories, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.000
are also laden with subtle hints at more mature&nbsp;
subjects, including riddles and numbers games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.160
critical thinking, philosophy, and local history.&nbsp;
Disney's Alice in Wonderland begins with Alice's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.120
older sister reading her excerpts from some&nbsp;
of that history. This scene even features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.480
quotes from a real-life historical text:&nbsp;
Havilland Chepnell's Short Course of History.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.920
"And even Stigand, the Archbishop of Canterbury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.840
agreed to meet with William&nbsp;
and offer him the crown."

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.000
This same passage makes an appearance in Lewis&nbsp;
Carroll's novel. In fact, the book was studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.800
by Alice Liddell, one of Carroll's dearest&nbsp;
child-friends, and the real-life inspiration for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.240
Alice in Wonderland. Throughout his life, Carroll&nbsp;
was friends with the children of his colleagues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.800
which was actually pretty normal for a Victorian&nbsp;
bachelor — but the nature of these friendships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.160
and Carroll's sexual identity has long been a&nbsp;
hotbed of debate among scholars. Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.440
the chosen excerpt is from a chapter examining&nbsp;
the rise and fall of the House of Normandy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.840
an era of history rife with noblewomen&nbsp;
and princesses who were named Alice.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.520
Sharper eyes will note a distinctive tower shape&nbsp;
in the background of the opening and closing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.800
scenes of Alice in Wonderland, which has left&nbsp;
many kids wondering if Alice lives in a castle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.600
In fact, this is none other than Christ Church&nbsp;
Cathedral, on the grounds of the prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.360
Oxford University in which Carroll lived,&nbsp;
studied, and worked for his entire adult life.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.240
Before he was an author, Lewis Carroll was&nbsp;
known best by his birth name, Charles Lutwidge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.440
Dodgson. He was a brilliant mathematician and&nbsp;
inventor, devoted scholar, and deacon of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.840
Anglican Church, all of which would influence his&nbsp;
fiction later in life. James Joyce, Salvador Dalí,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:41.360
numerous video game and comic book creators, and,&nbsp;
of course, Walt Disney have all been influenced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.880
Carroll's work. As a further homage to Alice in&nbsp;
Wonderland's real-world origins and influences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.480
a rowboat can be spotted in a pond in the first&nbsp;
scene. This was thoughtfully placed as an homage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.200
to the camaraderie-filled boat-rides which&nbsp;
helped inspire Carroll's fictional works.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.280
As a kid, watching Alice in Wonderland is&nbsp;
a simple, whimsical trip into a world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.880
technicolor fantasy. As an adult, however, one&nbsp;
can't help but derive deeper meaning from what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.880
might otherwise seem like nonsense — particularly&nbsp;
with regards to psychology and philosophy.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.400
Alice's adventures begin the moment she trails&nbsp;
her fingers across the surface of a garden pond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.600
In its reflection appears the White Rabbit,&nbsp;
running late as ever. This imagery, and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.040
ensuing fall down the rabbit hole, has been&nbsp;
interpreted as symbolizing the unconscious mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.680
which can only be accessed via dreams,&nbsp;
trances, or other similar states.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.320
Hidden within the unconscious mind, according&nbsp;
to some psychologists and philosophers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:47.280
are all the unknown aspects of oneself, including&nbsp;
repressed urges, fears, traumas, and beliefs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.800
Dreaming is one way for the unconscious mind to&nbsp;
communicate with the conscious mind, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.240
when one is undergoing a psychological imbalance&nbsp;
that can be harmful to one's well-being — sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.520
of like how a car's check engine light alerts&nbsp;
its owner to issues they might otherwise miss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.040
Carl Jung called these revelatory visions "Big&nbsp;
Dreams," and dedicated his life to the study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.960
and interpretation of symbolic dream imagery.&nbsp;
Likewise, the dream imagery in Alice in Wonderland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.400
has not gone unnoticed by academics, and&nbsp;
continues to be studied and discussed to this day.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.800
While older viewers will notice a prolific use&nbsp;
of dream symbolism in Alice in Wonderland, others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.640
hold a very different — and far more controversial&nbsp;
— perspective on the story's imagery: That Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.840
is actually under the influence of mind-altering&nbsp;
substances. Throughout the course of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.280
Alice consumes a variety of beverages, pastries,&nbsp;
and even mushrooms, all of which alter her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.200
perspective by causing her to grow or shrink in&nbsp;
size. The use of mushrooms in particular spurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:55.040
this theory on, which was initially popularized&nbsp;
by the counterculture movement of the 1960s.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.880
Most famously, the psychedelic rock band Jefferson&nbsp;
Airplane stoked the fires of this theory with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:07.200
release of their 1967 hit, "White Rabbit" — a&nbsp;
song which makes explicit reference to pills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.920
hookah-smoking, and being "off your head."

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.960
This track became an iconic anthem of&nbsp;
'60s counterculture, and ushered in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.640
slew of similar interpretations&nbsp;
from other artists and authors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.400
including Go Ask Alice, a fictional account of&nbsp;
a teen girl's spiral into addiction. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.160
Lewis Carroll has no documented&nbsp;
history of pervasive drug use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.840
and all indications are that he would actually&nbsp;
be rather appalled by this interpretation.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.480
Many animation studios, including&nbsp;
Disney, have become notorious for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.360
layering adult humor into their films —&nbsp;
and Alice in Wonderland is no exception.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.720
Early in the movie, Alice finds herself stuck&nbsp;
inside the White Rabbit's house after nibbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.000
on a pastry which makes her grow to monstrous&nbsp;
proportions. In an effort to prevent this from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.000
happening, the rabbit runs into the house —&nbsp;
and back out again, when Alice grows too large.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.200
Sharp eyes following his path will note a family&nbsp;
portrait lining the stairs — but this is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:11.360
ordinary domestic depiction. Whereas your average&nbsp;
family unit is composed of a handful of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.080
there are so many children and other relations&nbsp;
pictured in the Rabbit's family that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.280
single frame runs the entire length of the stairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.960
The kids won't pick up on it, but&nbsp;
older viewers will be more than aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.840
that this is a sneaky, risqué reference&nbsp;
to the phrase "multiplying like rabbits."

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.040
"Golden Afternoon" is one of the most&nbsp;
beautiful songs from Alice in Wonderland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:38.000
This delightful singalong is shared with&nbsp;
Alice by a group of anthropomorphic flowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.880
The gorgeous scenery they provide makes&nbsp;
this a high point of the movie — that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.640
until the flowers take a&nbsp;
shocking turn in their attitudes.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.440
Curious about their unexpected guest's origins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.400
they ask Alice what kind of garden she comes&nbsp;
from. But when she explains she doesn't come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.520
from a garden, her new friends assume she&nbsp;
has more uncouth origins as a wildflower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.720
and their attitudes take a very dark turn&nbsp;
indeed. They then go on to criticize Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.920
with rude statements clearly meant to cause her&nbsp;
emotional distress over her physical appearance.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.680
"Just look at those stems."&nbsp;
"Rather scrawny, I'd say."

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.880
When a sweet-natured baby rosebud&nbsp;
remarks that she thinks Alice is pretty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.920
her mother tells her to be quiet while covering&nbsp;
her mouth. The turbulence gets kicked up even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:29.360
more when Alice declares she's not a flower at&nbsp;
all, which drives the others to assume she is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.720
quote, "nothing more" than a "common weed."&nbsp;
This assumption stirs them into a frenzy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:40.160
eager to shoo Alice away before she "goes&nbsp;
to seed" and spreads "weeds in their bed."

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.160
Alice is right when she declares these flowers&nbsp;
could learn a few things about manners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.160
of course. And whether this is taken&nbsp;
as a reference to class snobbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.040
Alice's rebelliousness, or even body-shaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.840
21st century adults will know better that&nbsp;
what those flowers are saying just isn't cool.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.120
For as long as humans have existed, we've&nbsp;
marveled over the puzzling nature of who we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:06.560
are and how we came to be. This is a deeply&nbsp;
complex question, of course, and basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.840
every culture in human history has developed&nbsp;
its own theories in an attempt to answer it.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.000
On the surface, Alice's encounter with&nbsp;
the hookah-smoking Caterpillar is merely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.960
a string of silly puns — likewise, his&nbsp;
insistence on asking Alice who she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.680
could easily be interpreted as a simple&nbsp;
question from one stranger to another.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.800
However, some viewers might note hints of Eastern&nbsp;
philosophy present in the Caterpillar's character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.120
Schools of thought and religions from&nbsp;
the far East, like Taoism and Buddhism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.720
often interrogate concepts of an individual&nbsp;
"self," separate from the divine. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:45.520
many believe the sensation of self is a&nbsp;
hallucination, and hold to the philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:51.280
that all beings are manifestations of a&nbsp;
single energy source. Consider Zen koans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:56.560
which are thought exercises — often in the form&nbsp;
of stories or riddles — that are designed to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.600
the student out of stagnant ways of thinking&nbsp;
and stimulate enlightenment. Sound familiar?

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:03.600
"If you ask me…"

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.680
"You? Who are you?"

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.480
Since animation has permeated popular&nbsp;
culture over the last century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.600
viewers have come to identify characters by the&nbsp;
sound of their voice just as much as their outward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.800
appearance. And thanks to the many talented actors&nbsp;
who have turned to voice acting over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.200
these characters are brought to life in&nbsp;
ways that transcend the written word.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:36.480
Some voice actors are true masters of their craft,&nbsp;
capable of portraying dozens of characters for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.080
various features in their lifetimes, each with a&nbsp;
unique voice and cadence. One of those legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.800
talents was the great Sterling Holloway, who&nbsp;
voice-acted in more than one hundred radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:53.200
programs, TV shows, and animated films during&nbsp;
a prolific career spanning nearly five decades.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.640
Sterling took on numerous projects&nbsp;
for Disney during that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.800
voicing iconic characters like Sleepy&nbsp;
in Snow White and the Seven Dwarves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:06.560
Winnie the Pooh, and even the Cheshire Cat in&nbsp;
Alice in Wonderland. It's easy to miss as a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.760
but adult ears will find Sterling's&nbsp;
unique, tinny rasp very easy to identify.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.840
"I just wanted to ask you&nbsp;
which way I ought to go."

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.000
"Well, that depends… on where you want to get to."

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.320
During the climax to Alice in Wonderland,&nbsp;
Alice finds herself in the Red Queen's kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.760
And, to her dismay, Alice quickly learns&nbsp;
that this royal ruler has some serious anger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:37.680
issues. Switching between a calm, ladylike&nbsp;
demeanor, and maniacal, serial killer-level wrath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.040
the Red Queen carries her weight around with all&nbsp;
the grace of an elephant in a glassware shop.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.640
The fear of the queen is so deeply&nbsp;
instilled in her subjects, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.120
that Alice finds anthropomorphic&nbsp;
playing cards painting white roses red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.080
because they accidentally planted the wrong&nbsp;
color. When the Queen catches them in the act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.320
she demands their death by execution with&nbsp;
one of the story's most iconic lines.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.360
"Off with their heads!"

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.560
This phrase is a notable favorite of the&nbsp;
Red Queen, who expresses a repeated desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.880
to kill her own people. And then there&nbsp;
are the numerous acts of animal cruelty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.560
In a twisted version of croquet, the Queen uses&nbsp;
live animals in place of traditional equipment:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:28.800
Flamingos replace mallets, hedgehogs are used in&nbsp;
place of balls, and playing cards replace hoops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.640
It's all pretty dark stuff, even for Alice in&nbsp;
Wonderland, and as an adult it's hard not to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.080
particularly bad for the Red Queen's subjects.&nbsp;
Thankfully, Alice escapes the Red Queen's rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.840
returning home safe and sound by awaking from&nbsp;
what appears to be a dream. Or so it seems…

